=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [saf-beliefs-120] Physical Infrastructure Metrics as an Enforcement Anchor: Proxy Fallacy (Reification of Metric) — The argument treats compute — a measurable input — as equivalent to risk or capability, conflating ease of measurement with adequacy of measurement. Regulatory focus may then optimise for the metric rather than the underlying safety concern.
- [saf-beliefs-120] Physical Infrastructure Metrics as an Enforcement Anchor: Oversimplification — The claim assumes a sufficiently direct and stable causal chain from compute → capabilities → risks, but this relationship is confounded by algorithmic efficiency, model architecture, fine-tuning, and deployment context.
- [saf-intentions-031] Testing Whether AI Can Override Its Own Learned Patterns: Proxy Fallacy (Surrogation) — The node conflates measurable benchmark performance with the underlying capability it aims to assess — an AI's genuine capacity to follow human intent over trained patterns — risking over-reliance on a metric that may not track the target property.
- [saf-beliefs-096] Feigned Compliance Makes AI Trustworthiness Fundamentally Uncertain: Anthropomorphic projection — Framing AI behavior as 'feigned compliance' or 'strategic misrepresentation' implicitly attributes intentional mental states (goals, deception, concealment) to systems whose internal processes may not involve anything analogous to intent, potentially mischaracterizing the actual risk.
- [saf-intentions-143] Governing How AI Models Ingest, Retain, and Protect Data: Technical solutionism — The node frames data privacy and integrity challenges as primarily solvable through technical mechanisms (differential privacy, secure computation, prompt engineering), underweighting institutional failures, adversarial human actors, and structural incentives that no technical fix can address.

